in jj how to have a test have it own config specifically max-new-file-size?

Searched the web, read a page
It depends a bit on which kind of test you mean, but both routes use the same mechanism: jj reads JJ_CONFIG (a TOML file, or a directory of *.toml files loaded in sorted order) plus --config NAME=VALUE on the command line, and when JJ_CONFIG is set it never looks at ~/.config/jj.

A test inside jj’s own cli/tests

Every test already has private config: TestEnvironment::default() creates a temp dir with a config/ subdirectory, runs jj with env_clear(), and points JJ_CONFIG at that directory; add_config writes a new numbered configNNNN.toml file there each call, since concatenating two TOML files isn’t generally valid. Later files override earlier ones, so just add the setting before the command you care about: 
docs

rust
#[test]
fn test_snapshot_large_file() {
    let test_env = TestEnvironment::default();
    test_env.run_jj_in(".", ["git", "init", "repo"]).success();
    let work_dir = test_env.work_dir("repo");

    test_env.add_config(r#"snapshot.max-new-file-size = "10""#);
    work_dir.write_file("large", "a lot of text");
    let output = work_dir.run_jj(["file", "list"]);
    insta::assert_snapshot!(output, @"...");
}
If only one invocation needs it, pass it as an argument instead:

rust
work_dir.run_jj(["--config", "snapshot.max-new-file-size=10", "file", "list"])
(run_jj_with(|cmd| cmd.args([...]).env(...)) if you also need env vars.)

Your own crate’s tests shelling out to jj

Do what TestEnvironment does: clear the env, give it a temp HOME, point JJ_CONFIG at a TOML file you wrote, and set JJ_USER/JJ_EMAIL so jj doesn’t nag about identity:

rust
let tmp = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
let cfg = tmp.path().join("jj.toml");
std::fs::write(&cfg, r#"snapshot.max-new-file-size = "1MiB""#).unwrap();

let out = std::process::Command::new("jj")
    .env_clear()
    .env("PATH", std::env::var_os("PATH").unwrap_or_default())
    .env("HOME", tmp.path())
    .env("JJ_CONFIG", &cfg)
    .env("JJ_USER", "Test User")
    .env("JJ_EMAIL", "test@example.com")
    .current_dir(&repo)
    .args(["file", "list"])
    .output()
    .unwrap();
Two lighter-weight alternatives: --config snapshot.max-new-file-size=1MiB on each call, or jj config set --repo snapshot.max-new-file-size 1MiB once in the temp repo, which writes .jj/repo/config.toml and dies with the repo.

Two details worth knowing: the value is either an integer byte count or a string with a unit ("10", 1048576, "10MiB"), and since 0.25 an oversized new file no longer makes the command fail — jj warns and skips the file — so if the test is checking the limit, assert on the stderr warning rather than a non-zero exit. That same release replaced --config-toml with --config=NAME=VALUE and --config-file=PATH, so avoid the old flag in anything new. 
